### What is STAR and why does it work?

STAR is a structured framework that forces every behavioral answer to be specific, personal, and quantified. Without STAR, candidates ramble or hide behind 'we'.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class STARFramework {
    record STAR(String situation, String task, String action, String result) {}
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        STAR example = new STAR(
            "Transfer p99 was 2000ms, breaching 1000ms SLA",
            "I owned the performance of the transfer service",
            "Profiled, found N+1 query, batched + added index",
            "p99: 2000ms -> 180ms (91% improvement)"
        );
        System.out.println(example);
    }
}
```

### How long should each part be?

Roughly 10% Situation, 10% Task, 60% Action, 20% Result. Action is the heart because the interviewer is evaluating what YOU did.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class STARCalibration {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Situation: ~10% (set the scene briefly)");
        System.out.println("Task:      ~10% (your specific responsibility)");
        System.out.println("Action:    ~60% (what YOU did, the longest part)");
        System.out.println("Result:    ~20% (measurable outcome with a number)");
    }
}
```

### Why must you use 'I' instead of 'we'?

The interviewer is evaluating you, not your team. 'We decided' and 'we built' leave them unable to assess your personal contribution.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class IvsWe {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Weak: 'We improved the transfer service'");
        System.out.println("Strong: 'I profiled the endpoint, found the N+1 query, and batched it'");
        System.out.println("You can credit the team, but your actions must be visible.");
    }
}
```

### Why does the Result need a number?

Numbers make the story credible and memorable. 'It got faster' is forgettable. 'p99 dropped from 2000ms to 180ms' sticks.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class MeasurableResult {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(MeasurableResult.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Vague: 'Transfers got faster'");
        log.info("Specific: 'p99: 2000ms -> 180ms, 91% improvement'");
        log.info("The number is what the interviewer remembers.");
    }
}
```

### How do you practice a STAR story?

Write it out as structured data, then rehearse a 2-minute and a 5-minute version. Check that each element is filled in.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class STARPractice {
    record STAR(String s, String t, String a, String r) {}
    public static boolean isComplete(STAR star) {
        return !star.s().isBlank() && !star.t().isBlank() && !star.a().isBlank() && !star.r().isBlank();
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        STAR good = new STAR(
            "Transfer latency breaching SLA", "Owned perf fix",
            "Profiled, found N+1, batched + indexed", "91% improvement"
        );
        STAR bad = new STAR("Something was slow", "", "We fixed it", "It got better");
        System.out.println("Good complete? " + isComplete(good));
        System.out.println("Bad complete?  " + isComplete(bad));
    }
}
```

```text
Good complete? true
Bad complete?  false
```

### How does STAR apply to a banking example?

Situation: the transfer service at National Bank of India had a p99 of 2000ms. Task: I owned the performance. Action: I profiled and found an N+1 query, batched it, and added an index. Result: p99 dropped to 180ms.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class BankingSTAR {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("S: Transfer p99 = 2000ms, SLA = 1000ms, at National Bank of India");
        System.out.println("T: I owned the performance of the transfer service");
        System.out.println("A: Profiled endpoint, found N+1 on Rajesh's statement, batched + indexed");
        System.out.println("R: p99 = 180ms, 91% improvement, SLA met");
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes in STAR answers?

No personal action (hiding behind 'we'), no measurable result, and too much Situation drowning out the Action.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class STARMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Mistake 1: hiding behind 'we' -> interviewer cannot assess you");
        System.out.println("Mistake 2: no number in Result -> story is not credible");
        System.out.println("Mistake 3: 5 minutes of Situation, 30 seconds of Action -> inverted");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class STARFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Tell me more about the Action -> expand the 60% with detail");
        System.out.println("What was the team's role? -> credit them, but keep your actions visible");
        System.out.println("Would you do anything differently? -> show reflection and learning");
    }
}
```
